        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: नवनीतम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नवनीतम् (navanītam) — \"fresh butter\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नवनीतम् (navanītam) is butter fresh off the churn, and its first piece is already yours."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first piece, nava, is \"new\" — the word you learned beside महत् and अल्प. The second, nīta, looks like \"brought, led\", from the nī inside नयति, but CDIAL lists a separate old nīta that already meant fresh butter, so the \"newly brought\" reading is a guess. Either way, this is the butter that comes fresh up out of the दधि (dadhi)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is the butter of the Kṛṣṇa stories, stolen by the handful, and Hindi navnīt is still a name people give a child."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Navanīta is a compound of nava, \"new\", and nīta, which CDIAL 7545 lists on its own as \"fresh butter\" (CDIAL 7003). It lives on as Marathi loṇī, \"butter\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Butter, named as the new thing that comes up out of the curd."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
