            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did navanītam mean, and what did śākam mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: मोदकः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मोदकः (modakaḥ) — \"a sweet dumpling\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मोदकः (modakaḥ) is a sweet — the round steamed dumpling put before a guest, and before a god."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is usually tied to mud, \"to be glad\", so the name of the sweet is the name of the gladness it makes. आनन्दः, the joy you offered at a first meeting, reaches for the same idea by a different root."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Set one in front of an अतिथिः and the word does its own explaining."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Modaka first meant \"gladdening\" (MW), and CDIAL 10354 links it, with a question mark, to the root √mud, \"to be glad\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five things from a kitchen: curd, sugar, butter, greens, a sweet."
            }
          ]
        },
